# Display width (section 8): terminal cells by grapheme cluster and East Asian width, never characters or bytes.
#
# WRITTEN HERE, IN TERM, BECAUSE THE STDLIB HAS NEITHER. `@term/base/text/unicode` counts grapheme clusters
# (`grapheme-count`) but cannot split text into them, and nothing in the stdlib knows a character's East Asian width.
# Both are logged as gaps in note/term/plan/silent-defects-third-batch.md. The tables below are the Unicode 15.1
# ranges that decide a width, and the clustering is the part of UAX #29 a terminal's width depends on: a base
# character and everything that extends it (combining marks, variation selectors, skin tones, tag characters, the
# vowel and final jamo of a Hangul syllable), emoji joined by ZWJ, and regional indicators in pairs. Pure Term over
# runes, so it is the same answer on every backend, which a native segmenter per platform would not be.
#
# A GRAIN is one cluster with its width. Wrapping, cropping and measuring all work in grains, so a span never lands
# half a character off and a cut never splits an emoji.
#
# NEUTRALIZING. A subject, a message or a child's output is somebody else's text, and a control character in it can
# move the cursor, clear the screen or recolor everything after it. `neutralize` makes every control visible instead
# (`␛` for ESC, `\x9b` for a C1 control) and every bidirectional override too, so a file name cannot reorder the line
# it is printed on. `strip-escapes` removes whole escape sequences from quoted output, which section 7 says loses its
# own colors.

load @term/base/text/unicode
  find to-runes
  find rune-to-text

load @term/base/text
  find char-count

load ./standard
  find standard
  find symbol-rule

form grain
  link value, like text
  link wide, like number

# characters that print nothing and join the cluster before them: combining marks, enclosing marks, format
# characters, variation selectors, skin-tone modifiers, tag characters, and the medial and final Hangul jamo
